      "thinking": "The real fault becomes clear: when the cache port changes to 6380, all lookups fail and latency spikes from 26ms to 190ms\u2014not because the cache itself is critical, but because the fallback path relies on expensive O(n\u00b2) computations for average and typical spend per item, exposing a latent performance bug that the cache had been masking.\n\nBoth `average_spend_per_item` and `typical_spend_per_item` recompute in O(n\u00b2), but the test comments and docstring for typical_spend seem to point at it as the deliberately planted expensive path \u2014 the docstring even hints \"cost follows the shape of the code rather than anything it's waiting on.\" That's a strong signal it's the intended hot spot to fix, though the lifetime-average path also matters since it's the one live for all traffic.\n\nI'm leaning toward fixing both to be linear since that's the safest route, and adding tests that assert bounded work rather than relying on timers. I need to check how Purchase objects are structured in accounts.py to figure out how to count operations for a performance-style test.\n\nI could subclass int to count comparisons, since min uses `<` and remove uses `==`. The quadratic current approach would rack up far more comparisons than a sorted/statistics-based O(n log n) fix, so I can assert the comparison count stays under some bound like n*n/10 to distinguish the two implementations.\n\nAlso worth checking `remaining.remove(min(remaining))` \u2014 since list.remove checks identity first before equality, the min element itself may be found quickly by identity, but earlier non-matching elements still trigger __eq__ calls, so both __lt__ and __eq__ counts matter. For average_spend_per_item, I'll track price_cents accesses through a wrapper class, and since the Account dataclass likely doesn't validate purchase types, passing wrapper objects instead of real objects should work fine.\n\nFor fixing average_spend_per_item, I want to keep summing price_cents across purchases and dividing by count, which preserves the original behavior including raising ZeroDivisionError on empty purchases, but makes it linear instead of quadratic. Let me go read accounts.py to confirm the actual implementation.\n\n",
